WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.440
Episode 6 of "Loki", "For All Time, Always," has&nbsp;
introduced Kang the Conqueror, and will probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.080
change the face of the MCU forever. Here's&nbsp;
the ending of "Loki" Episode 6 explained.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.120
The Citadel at the end of time is a truly&nbsp;
bizarre place, filled with ruined statues of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.040
the Time Keepers and ominous looking details.&nbsp;
The creepy design of the building looks more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.640
like something out of "Harry Potter" than&nbsp;
the MCU, which makes it even weirder when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.400
Miss Minutes appears out of nowhere. She's&nbsp;
not the overarching villain of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.560
but she's certainly not good either. As&nbsp;
it turns out, the animated clock is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.560
direct line to a mysterious individual&nbsp;
she refers to as "He Who Remains."

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.800
Once again, the MCU has taken something from&nbsp;
the comics and repurposed it for the ongoing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.440
franchise. He Who Remains is in charge of the&nbsp;
Time Variance Authority in the source material,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:49.200
even meeting Thor and Jane Foster in&nbsp;
"Thor #245" back in 1976. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.440
when he arrives on screen in "Loki," it's&nbsp;
instantly clear that He Who Remains in the MCU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.600
is actually a variant of Kang the Conqueror,&nbsp;
played by none other than Jonathan Majors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.320
who recently broke out as the&nbsp;
star of "Lovecraft Country."

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.280
Majors' casting as Kang was previously revealed&nbsp;
by Deadline in September 2020 in a report claiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.200
he'll be the villain in "Ant-Man and The Wasp:&nbsp;
Quantumania" — although how he'll go up against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.680
the MCU's tiniest hero remains to be seen.&nbsp;
If you're up on your MCU casting news it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.320
instantly clear who this mysterious, purple-robed&nbsp;
individual is once Majors' character steps out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.640
an elevator to meet Sylvie and Loki. Majors&nbsp;
clearly has a lot of fun with the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.480
and he has a pretty chaotic presence&nbsp;
which rivals the God of Mischief himself.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.960
However, in yet another twist, he's not exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.840
the version of Kang the Conqueror&nbsp;
that fans might be familiar with.

00:01:32.932 --> 00:01:35.840
While other MCU Disney+ shows like&nbsp;
"WandaVision" and "The Falcon and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.000
The Winter Soldier" featured action-packed&nbsp;
finales, "Loki" takes a quieter approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.160
It mainly revolves around the universe-breaking&nbsp;
conversation between this Kang Variant, Loki,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.800
and Sylvie about the true nature of the TVA,&nbsp;
the Sacred Timeline, and the Multiverse War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.160
Essentially, it prepares audiences&nbsp;
for Jonathan Majors' ongoing role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.920
within the MCU as Kang the Conqueror,&nbsp;
because as He Who Remains explains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.000
he's but one of the countless variants of the&nbsp;
villain that exist throughout the multiverse.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.040
Essentially, He Who Remains is using the TVA and&nbsp;
Alioth to keep the Sacred Timeline flowing as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.760
thinks it should, which ensures that the universe&nbsp;
doesn't fall into yet another Multiverse War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:14.160
The war itself was, once upon a time, waged&nbsp;
by different Kang variants, who originally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.920
united for the good of every universe only&nbsp;
to ultimately betray one another and fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.880
for one timeline's supremacy. In the comics,&nbsp;
this alliance was called the Council of Kangs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.040
and they've been around for centuries. He Who&nbsp;
Remains even alludes to this in the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.680
saying that he's been called many names over time:

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:37.680
"A ruler, a conqueror, He Who Remains, a jerk."

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.560
Interestingly, the Kang we see in "Loki"&nbsp;
bears more of a resemblance to Immortus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.640
the older, somewhat wiser Kang variant who&nbsp;
pops up in the comics as sort of a cosmic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.680
gardener of the Marvel time stream. Regardless,&nbsp;
where do Loki and Sylvie fit into all of this?

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.240
He Who Remains says he's been pulling the strings&nbsp;
on them from the very start, dictating their lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:00.160
so that they both arrive at this meeting.&nbsp;
However, the seemingly all-knowing villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.680
doesn't actually, well, know it all. His knowledge&nbsp;
of the timeline only goes up to a certain point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.400
stating that once they cross that threshold,&nbsp;
anything Loki and Sylvie do is out of his control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.600
That's when the sacred timeline begins splintering&nbsp;
all around the Citadel. Coincidence? Surely not.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.640
This revelation forces Loki and Sylvie into&nbsp;
a fight as they grapple with the choice of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.440
killing He Who Remains for creating the TVA and&nbsp;
snatching free-will away from the timeline...or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.360
taking over the TVA themselves. Sylvie&nbsp;
is all for killing him straight away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.120
because her goal from the start of the show has&nbsp;
been to tear down the TVA and those who run it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.800
So it's understandable that she wants revenge&nbsp;
for the life that was snatched away from her.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.280
However, Loki clearly recognizes the bigger&nbsp;
potential danger of another Multiverse War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.880
and the other Kang variants that are out&nbsp;
there — leading to a brief duel. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.760
romantic tension between these two is palpable,&nbsp;
especially when Sylvie passionately kisses Loki.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.360
In fact, Loki has clearly pushed aside&nbsp;
his own goals of domination for her.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:55.600
"I just want you to be okay!"

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.000
Unfortunately, the kiss was self-serving&nbsp;
for Sylvie, who uses it to distract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.680
Loki in order to hurl him through a&nbsp;
time-door. She may call herself Sylvie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.640
but she's still a Loki —&nbsp;
and that means manipulation.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.240
From the very beginning, Sylvie's been on a&nbsp;
journey of her own and will not compromise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:15.840
for anything or anyone. Ultimately, she stabs He&nbsp;
Who Remains, and he dies in his chair. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.160
this isn't even close to the end for Jonathan&nbsp;
Majors. Before he dies, the villain whispers,

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.280
"See you soon."

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.960
Basically, get ready to see a whole&nbsp;
ton of different Kangs in the MCU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.360
And that's the last we see of Sylvie...for now.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.960
The TVA took a bit of a backseat in this episode,&nbsp;
but clearly the agency will never be the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:40.480
again, as Hunter B-15 finds the original Ravonna&nbsp;
Renslayer in a school in 2018 Ohio to convince the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.200
rest of her fellow hunters that everyone in the&nbsp;
TVA is actually a variant. It's a pretty shocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.280
revelation, but clearly the TVA hasn't torn&nbsp;
itself apart because they're all still watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.240
the Sacred Timeline splinter beyond repair by the&nbsp;
end of the episode. Meanwhile the TVA's version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.800
of Ravonna disappeared early in the episode to&nbsp;
search for "free-will" — whatever that means.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.800
When Loki crashes back through the time door,&nbsp;
he frantically tries to warn Mobius about He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.640
Who Remains, and the threat of an impending&nbsp;
Multiverse War. But when he finally tracks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:10.320
his friend down and begins talking, Mobius&nbsp;
stops him and asks him what his name is.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.680
"Who are you? What's your name?"

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.640
Wow. Clearly, Loki has ended up in&nbsp;
an alternate version of the TVA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:20.480
and a Variant Kang has already messed with&nbsp;
the timeline, changing events in the past to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.040
change the status quo in Loki's present. That's&nbsp;
confirmed by the towering statue of the villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:29.280
in a pretty comic book-accurate version of&nbsp;
his costume at the center of the TVA complex.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.160
As fans may already know, Kang's reach spans&nbsp;
across millennia, so changing the past to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:39.520
put himself in a supreme position of power isn't&nbsp;
much of a surprise. Luckily, with "Loki" Season 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:43.920
already greenlit, we'll have plenty of time to see&nbsp;
how the God of Mischief tackles this new timeline.

